---
import ToolLayout from '@/layouts/ToolLayout.astro'
import MeanMedianMode from '@/components/tools/calc2/MeanMedianMode.tsx'
---

<ToolLayout slug="mean-median-mode-calculator">
  <MeanMedianMode client:load />

  <Fragment slot="explainer">
    <h2>How the mean, median and mode calculator works</h2>
    <p>
      Paste or type a list of numbers. The mean, median and mode calculator sorts them, then finds the
      three averages, the range, the count and the sum.
    </p>
    <pre><code>mean    sum of the values ÷ how many there are
median  the middle value when sorted; for an even count, the mean of the two middle values
mode    the value that occurs most often (there can be several, or none)
range   largest − smallest</code></pre>

    <h2>Worked example</h2>
    <p>
      For 4, 8, 15, 16, 23, 42, 8, 15, 8: the sum is 139 and there are 9 values, so the mean is
      139 ÷ 9 = 15.44. Sorted, the list is 4, 8, 8, 8, 15, 15, 16, 23, 42; the 5th value, 15, is the
      median. 8 occurs three times, so it is the mode. The range is 42 − 4 = 38.
    </p>

    <h2>Which average to use</h2>
    <p>
      The mean uses every value, so one large value pulls it up: 42 lifts the mean above the median here.
      The median ignores how far the extreme values are, so it suits incomes and house prices. The mode
      suits categories and counts, such as the most common shoe size.
    </p>

    <h2>Limits</h2>
    <p>
      Values with up to 9 decimals are added exactly, so 0.1 + 0.2 gives 0.3. The dot plot stacks equal
      values; very close values that are not equal sit side by side.
    </p>
  </Fragment>
</ToolLayout>
